Tests
=====

Sanity tests for the core functionality, run by CI (.github/workflows/ci.yml)
on every push and pull request:

  pio run                  build the firmware for all boards
  pio test -e native       host tests of the firmware code (below)
  cd web-client && npm ci && npm test
                           web client (chain.js and the page in jsdom)

Host tests (PlatformIO `native` environment, needs gcc/g++ on the PC)
--------------------------------------------------------------------

The hardware-independent firmware sources (src/crypto, src/u2f.cpp,
src/buttons.cpp, src/utils.cpp, SecureStorage, src/se) are compiled for
the PC and linked against stand-ins for Arduino, the I2C EEPROM, the
ATECC508A, USB HID and the OLED (test/libs/HostStubs). Expected values
come from independent sources: the BIP39/BIP44/BIP84 test vectors, embit
and eth-account.

  test_wallet   BIP39 words; BIP84 SegWit and BIP44 legacy/ETH addresses of
                the "abandon ... about" test mnemonic; output descriptor;
                root bytes for the secure element; Ethereum transaction
                signature; ERC-20 recognition; PSBT review, signing
                (signature verified) and merging; malformed PSBTs refused.
  test_atecc    ATECC508A driver against a fake chip on the stub I2C bus:
                CRC reference packets, wake, Info, config read and decoding,
                retries, timeouts, only read-only commands sent; invariants
                of the slot map (nothing lockable, every slot rewritable via
                the PUK, secrets never readable, known encodings) and the
                config built from it. The encrypted commands are verified
                on the chip itself by env:seprov_* (command 'v').
  test_se_wallet  wallet secrets in the secure element (src/se/se_wallet.cpp)
                against a fake ATECC508A that enforces the slot rules
                (encrypted read/write only with the right key, write MAC,
                MAC, counters): create, unlock, wrong PIN count, PUK needed
                for a new wallet, PIN change, wipe, factory reset, FIDO2
                secret and counter, randomness. The same flows run on the
                real chip with env:seprov_* ('WALLET TEST').
  test_u2f      FIDO2 over emulated USB HID: register, sign in, verify the
                attestation and assertion signatures, sign in again after a
                reboot, NO button denies, wrong site / no wallet refused.

Windows has no gcc by default; PlatformIO's MinGW toolchain works:

  pio pkg install -g -t platformio/toolchain-gccmingw32
  set PATH=%USERPROFILE%\.platformio\packages\toolchain-gccmingw32\bin;%PATH%
  pio test -e native

Web client fixtures
-------------------

web-client/test/fixtures.json holds reference vectors computed with rlp,
eth_abi and embit. Regenerate them with test/tools/make_web_fixtures.py
(see the instructions in that file).
